  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 Days...\n\nWith plenty of moisture left over from the weekend, and another\nclear night with calm winds, fog development is forecast again \nthis morning. The area of concern is the same locations that saw \ndense fog Monday morning. With dew points in the 40s near\nLivingston, along with the surface high over YNP, it will \nprobably be difficult to see any fog at in the immediate \nfoothills, however low stratus is a possibility through the \nmorning hours. Back near Billings, dew points are in the low 50s \nand temps are quickly dropping. While the scale of fog may not be\nas widespread and dense as last night, it should cover roughly \nthe same geographic footprint. Nearly all forecast model soundings \nshow a good fog setup at Billings this morning, some even show a \ngood setup down in Sheridan as well. \n\nToday, temperatures will be a touch above average with many \nplaces seeing highs in the upper 70s. Tonight (after 00z) a \nshortwave is forecast to move out of the southwest through our \narea. In recent model runs this feature has actually been pulled a\nlittle bit westwards which would give nearly all of our CWA a \nchance at some precipitation. Showers and storms will be a quick \nhitter overnight into Wednesday. With how fast the system will be \nmoving, most of the precipitation wil be out of our forecast area \nby the afternoon Wednesday. PWATS in the plains may exceed 1\" \nwhich would allow some cells to produce locally heavy rainfall. \nFor the most part though, any rainfall accumulation will be light\nwith this event. \n\nThe rest of the work week will be primarily dry as we remain just\nwithin the influence of the Great Plains ridge. This will also \nallow temps to remain in the upper 70s and low 80s for highs with \nlows only dropping into the 50s. \n\nThe forecast for this weekend has notably changed in the past 24\nhours. The main shortwave coming in off of the Pacific is now\nbeing pushed farther north in most model runs. This would be good\nnews for most as that means that the likelihood of a strong wind \nevent Saturday would be much lower. There is also another feature,\nsimilar to the midweek one actually, that rides the southern \nbranch of the jet up through WY Friday afternoon. This would help \nincrease precipitation chances if this were to pan out. As we saw \nin the past day though, this forecast could still change quite a \nbit in the next 4 to 5 days.\n\nBoth the ECMWF and the GFS keep our area in a progressive pattern\nthrough the first part of next week with several shortwaves coming\noff of the Pacific and moving through Montana. The main question \nfor next week's forecast will be what happens with a handful of \ntropical systems. The remnants of Typhoon Dujuan will be \nabsorbed into the polar jet stream in the next day or two. There \ncould also be a few major hurricanes that form in the Eastern \nPacific over the next week which could alter the tropical branch \nof the jet and/or advect a lot of moisture into the mid-latitudes.\nWMR",
